/* phase F und_r21: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["21a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["21a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"21a1.1","q":"What does the arc-length technique say, why is its integral improper at 1, and why does it converge all the same?","a":"the arc of the unit circle from (1, 0) to the point above x has length L(x) = ∫ₓ¹ dt/√(1 − t²); the bottom is 0 at t = 1; it converges because 1/√(1 − t²) ≤ 1/√(1 − t) there, and that integral converges","w":["L(x) = ∫ₓ¹ √(1 − t²) dt; it is improper because the top is 0 at 1; it converges because the area is bounded","L(x) = ∫ₓ¹ dt/√(1 − t²); it is improper because the interval is endless; it converges because the circle is closed","L(x) = ∫ₓ¹ dt/√(1 − t²); it is improper at 1 and does not converge, so the length is taken from a picture"],"why":"The curve is steep at (1, 0), but not steep enough to make the length endless."},
 {"u":"21a2.1","q":"What is π, from here on, and why is around ÷ across the same for every circle?","a":"π = L(−1), the length of the upper half of the circle of radius 1; a circle of radius r is the stretch of that one by r, and a stretch by r multiplies every length by r, so around/across = 2πr/2r = π","w":["π is the number 22/7; the quotient is the same because it was measured on many circles","π = L(−1), the length of the whole circle of radius 1; the quotient is the same because all circles are congruent","π = L(0), a quarter of the circle; the quotient is the same because a stretch keeps every length"],"why":"The lower half is the flip of the upper half, so the whole circle of radius 1 has length 2π."},
 {"u":"21a3.1","q":"What does the arc-picks-a-point technique say, why is the point only one, and what is an angle, from here on?","a":"for each θ between 0 and π exactly one point of the upper half circle has arc θ from (1, 0), since L is continuous and falls from π to 0 as x rises; an angle is the length θ of the arc it cuts, with the radius as unit","w":["for each θ there are two such points, one on each side; an angle is how wide the corner looks","for each θ exactly one point has arc θ, since L rises with x; an angle is the area of the slice it cuts","for each point there is exactly one θ, but some θ have no point; an angle is the opening e₁ · e₂"],"why":"L′(x) = −1/√(1 − x²) is negative, and L(0) = π/2."},
 {"u":"21a4.1","q":"What does the eighth-arc technique say, and why does every value of π you write carry a width?","a":"π = 4 ∫₀^{1/√2} dt/√(1 − t²), an integral with no trouble at either end; π is one exact number, but no list of digits and no fraction is π, so each written value is a fence between a lower and an upper sum","w":["π = 8 ∫₀¹ dt/√(1 − t²); π carries a width because it changes from circle to circle","π = 4 ∫₀^{1/√2} dt/√(1 − t²); π carries a width because it is not one fixed number","π = 4 ∫₀^{1/√2} dt/√(1 − t²); a written value carries no width once it has six digits"],"why":"With 4 strips, π is between 3.015 and 3.309; you choose the width when you choose how finely to cut."},
 {"u":"21a5.1","q":"Why are \"π = 22/7\" and \"L(x) = ∫ₓ¹ √(1 − t²) dt\" moves to watch out for?","a":"22/7 is a guess with no fence, a little too big; the second integral is an area, and a length needs √(1 + f′²)","w":["22/7 is a little too small; the second integral is right for the lower half only","22/7 is exact for circles of radius 7; the second integral leaves out a factor 2","22/7 has a fence of width 0; the second integral is the length of the whole circle"],"why":"1 + f′(t)² = 1/(1 − t²) for the upper half circle."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["21b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["21b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"21b1.1","q":"What are cos θ and sin θ, for 0 ≤ θ ≤ π, and which point of the circle is (cos θ, sin θ)?","a":"cos θ is the one x with L(x) = θ, and sin θ = √(1 − cos² θ); the point reached by walking θ around the circle from (1, 0), anticlockwise","w":["cos θ = L(θ) and sin θ = 1 − L(θ); the point at height θ","cos θ is the one x with L(x) = θ, and sin θ = 1 − cos θ; the point reached by walking θ clockwise from (0, 1)","cos θ is facing over longest in a right triangle, which is defined only for θ below π/2"],"why":"L(1) = 0, L(0) = π/2 and L(−1) = π give the points (1, 0), (0, 1) and (−1, 0)."},
 {"u":"21b2.1","q":"How are cos θ and sin θ defined for θ between π and 2π?","a":"the lower half is the flip of the upper half over the floor: cos θ = cos(2π − θ) and sin θ = −sin(2π − θ)","w":["cos θ = −cos(2π − θ) and sin θ = sin(2π − θ)","cos θ = cos(θ − π) and sin θ = sin(θ − π)","they are not defined past π"],"why":"The flip keeps the across and changes the sign of the up."},
 {"u":"21b3.1","q":"How are cos θ and sin θ defined for every real θ, and what does the whole-turns technique say?","a":"by the remainder of θ in [0, 2π) after taking out whole turns 2πk; adding a whole turn changes nothing: cos(θ + 2π) = cos θ and sin(θ + 2π) = sin θ","w":["by the remainder of θ in [0, π); adding a half turn changes nothing","by θ itself, as a power series; adding a whole turn changes the sign of both","only for θ ≥ 0; a negative θ has no cos or sin"],"why":"A negative θ is a walk clockwise: cos(−π/2) = 0 and sin(−π/2) = −1."},
 {"u":"21b4.1","q":"What does the thirds-of-the-half technique say, which flips prove it, and which arcs have points that can be written with square roots?","a":"(1/2, √3/2) and (−1/2, √3/2) cut the upper half circle into three arcs of π/3; the flip across the line through (0, 0) and (1/2, √3/2) swaps the first two, and the flip across the upright axis swaps the first and last; these, the sixths, the eighth and the quarters can be written so, and most arcs cannot","w":["(1/2, √3/2) and (−1/2, √3/2) cut the half into three arcs of π/3, by measuring; every arc's point can be written with square roots","(√3/2, 1/2) and (−√3/2, 1/2) cut the half into three arcs of π/3; the flip over the floor proves it","(1/2, 1/2) and (−1/2, 1/2) cut the half into three arcs of π/3; two quarter turns prove it"],"why":"So cos(π/3) = 1/2 and sin(π/3) = √3/2, and by the flip across y = x, cos(π/6) = √3/2."},
 {"u":"21b5.1","q":"What does the opening-is-cosine technique say, and why? What does the next-and-facing technique say?","a":"the opening of an angle of size θ is cos θ: slide the corner to O and turn e₁ onto (1, 0), and e₂ is the point at arc θ, so e₁ · e₂ = cos θ; in a right triangle with longest side c, the side next to θ is c cos θ and the side facing it is c sin θ","w":["the opening of an angle of size θ is sin θ; the side next to θ is c sin θ and the side facing it is c cos θ","the opening of an angle of size θ is θ itself; the side next to θ is c/cos θ","the opening is cos θ, because moves change dot products by cos θ; the side facing θ is c tan θ"],"why":"Moves keep lengths of paths, so the arc θ is kept, and turns keep dot products."},
 {"u":"21b6.1","q":"Why are \"sin θ = √(1 − cos² θ) for every θ\" and \"cos 2π = cos 0, so 2π = 0\" moves to watch out for?","a":"the root holds only on the upper half, since below the floor sin is negative; cos takes each value many times, so it cannot be run backwards without a choice","w":["the root holds only below the floor; cos is one-to-one, so 2π = 0 follows","the root never holds; cos 2π is not cos 0","the root holds for every θ; the fault is that 2π is not an angle"],"why":"sin(3π/2) = −1, and the root would give +1."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["21c"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["21c"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"21c1.1","q":"What does the circle technique for cos and sin say, why does it hold below the floor too, and what does sin² θ mean?","a":"for every θ, cos² θ + sin² θ = 1; the lower half flips the sign of sin, which squaring forgets, and whole turns change nothing; sin² θ means (sin θ)²","w":["for every θ, cos θ + sin θ = 1; below the floor both change sign; sin² θ means sin(sin θ)","for θ on the upper half only, cos² θ + sin² θ = 1; sin² θ means sin(θ²)","for every θ, cos² θ − sin² θ = 1; it holds below the floor because cos is even"],"why":"It is the right-triangle technique of 18a, laid on the across and the up."},
 {"u":"21c2.1","q":"What does the quarters technique say, and what fixes the sign of each coordinate?","a":"the signs of cos θ and sin θ are fixed by the quarter the walk ends in: cos falls from 1 to −1 on [0, π], passing 0 only at π/2, sin is positive strictly between 0 and π, and the lower half flips the sign of sin and keeps cos","w":["cos and sin are both positive in every quarter; only tan changes sign","cos is positive on the upper half and negative on the lower; sin is positive on the right half","the signs are fixed by whether θ is positive or negative"],"why":"First quarter + +, second − +, third − −, fourth + −."},
 {"u":"21c3.1","q":"What are tan θ, sec θ, cot θ and csc θ, when is each defined, and what does the tangent-is-steepness technique say?","a":"tan θ = sin θ/cos θ and sec θ = 1/cos θ where cos θ ≠ 0; cot θ = cos θ/sin θ and csc θ = 1/sin θ where sin θ ≠ 0; tan θ is the slope of the line from the centre to the point at θ","w":["tan θ = cos θ/sin θ and sec θ = 1/sin θ, defined everywhere; tan θ is the length of the arc","tan θ = sin θ/cos θ and sec θ = 1/cos θ where sin θ ≠ 0; tan θ is the height of the point at θ","tan θ = sin θ · cos θ; it is defined everywhere; tan θ is the slope of the circle at θ"],"why":"That line meets the upright line x = 1 at the point (1, tan θ)."},
 {"u":"21c4.1","q":"What does the tangent-secant technique say, how is it proved, and what is it in the picture?","a":"wherever cos θ ≠ 0, 1 + tan² θ = sec² θ: divide the circle technique through by cos² θ; it is the right triangle (0, 0), (1, 0), (1, tan θ), with sides 1 and |tan θ| and longest side |sec θ|","w":["1 + sec² θ = tan² θ: multiply the circle technique by cos² θ","tan² θ + sec² θ = 1: divide the circle technique by sin² θ","1 + tan² θ = sec² θ for every θ: it needs no dividing"],"why":"Dividing by sin² θ instead gives 1 + cot² θ = csc² θ."},
 {"u":"21c5.1","q":"Why are \"cos θ = √(1 − sin² θ)\" and \"tan(π/2) = ∞\" moves to watch out for?","a":"the root holds only where cos θ ≥ 0, since the sign comes from the quarter; cos(π/2) = 0, so tan is not defined there at all","w":["the root holds only where sin θ ≥ 0; tan(π/2) = 0","the root holds for every θ; tan(π/2) = 1","the root never holds; tan(π/2) is a very large number"],"why":"cos π = −1, and the root would give +1."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["21d"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["21d"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"21d1.1","q":"What does the path-length-kept technique say, and why does a turn keep the length of a path?","a":"a slide, a turn or a flip carries every path to a path of the same length; a turn sends the velocity to its own turn, which has the same length, so the speeds are equal and so are the areas under them","w":["only a slide keeps the length of a path; a turn stretches the outer paths","a turn keeps the length because it keeps the two ends of the path","a slide, a turn or a flip keeps the length of straight paths only"],"why":"Q′(t) = R(P′(t)), and |R(v)| = |v| (20d2)."},
 {"u":"21d2.1","q":"What does the turning-adds-arcs technique say, and why does the image arc run anticlockwise?","a":"the turn by the pair (cos B, sin B) sends the point at arc A to the point at arc A + B; a turn keeps which way round, so the image of the anticlockwise arc is anticlockwise","w":["the turn by (cos B, sin B) sends the point at arc A to the point at arc AB; a turn reverses which way round","the turn by (cos B, sin B) sends the point at arc A to the point at arc A − B; the arc runs clockwise","the turn by (cos B, sin B) sends the point at arc A to the point at arc A + B; the image runs anticlockwise because every arc does"],"why":"The image arc starts at the point at B and has length A: walk B, then A more."},
 {"u":"21d3.1","q":"What does the sum technique say, and from which formula does it come?","a":"cos(A + B) = cos A cos B − sin A sin B and sin(A + B) = sin A cos B + cos A sin B; apply the turn formula (ax − by, bx + ay) with (a, b) = (cos B, sin B) to (cos A, sin A)","w":["cos(A + B) = cos A + cos B and sin(A + B) = sin A + sin B; from the slide formula","cos(A + B) = cos A cos B + sin A sin B and sin(A + B) = sin A cos B − cos A sin B; from the flip formula","cos(A + B) = cos A cos B and sin(A + B) = sin A sin B; from multiplying the coordinates"],"why":"The turn by B sends the point at A to the point at A + B (21d2), and the coordinates agree."},
 {"u":"21d4.1","q":"What does the double technique say, how does it come from the sum technique, and what is tan(A + B)?","a":"cos 2A = cos² A − sin² A and sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A: put B = A; tan(A + B) = (tan A + tan B)/(1 − tan A tan B)","w":["cos 2A = 2 cos A and sin 2A = 2 sin A; tan(A + B) = tan A + tan B","cos 2A = cos² A + sin² A and sin 2A = sin² A; tan(A + B) = (tan A + tan B)/(1 + tan A tan B)","cos 2A = 1 − 2cos² A and sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A; tan(A + B) = tan A tan B"],"why":"With the circle technique, cos 2A = 2cos² A − 1 = 1 − 2sin² A."},
 {"u":"21d5.1","q":"Why are \"cos(A + B) = cos A + cos B\" and \"cos(A + B) = cos A cos B + sin A sin B\" moves to watch out for?","a":"with A = B = π/3, cos(2π/3) = −1/2 but 1/2 + 1/2 = 1; and the sign is wrong, since the turn formula has ax − by","w":["the first holds only for small A and B; the second is the right formula","the first is right when A = B; the second is the formula for sin(A + B)","both hold on the upper half and fail below the floor"],"why":"cos A cos B + sin A sin B is cos(A − B)."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["21e"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["21e"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"21e1.1","q":"What does the reversal technique say, which flip proves it, and what are cos, sin and tan in 9e's words?","a":"cos(−θ) = cos θ and sin(−θ) = −sin θ; the flip over the floor carries the arc θ to an arc of the same length running clockwise; cos is even, and sin and tan are odd","w":["cos(−θ) = −cos θ and sin(−θ) = sin θ; the flip across the upright axis; cos is odd and sin is even","cos(−θ) = cos θ and sin(−θ) = sin θ; the half turn; all three are even","cos(−θ) = cos θ and sin(−θ) = −sin θ; the flip across y = x; cos and tan are even"],"why":"F₀(cos θ, sin θ) = (cos θ, −sin θ)."},
 {"u":"21e2.1","q":"What does the difference technique say, and how does it come from the sum technique?","a":"cos(A − B) = cos A cos B + sin A sin B and sin(A − B) = sin A cos B − cos A sin B; write A − B as A + (−B) and use reversal on −B","w":["cos(A − B) = cos A − cos B and sin(A − B) = sin A − sin B; subtract the sum technique twice","cos(A − B) = cos A cos B − sin A sin B, the same as for a sum; cos is even","cos(A − B) = cos A cos B + sin A sin B and sin(A − B) = cos A sin B − sin A cos B; swap A and B"],"why":"cos(−B) = cos B and sin(−B) = −sin B."},
 {"u":"21e3.1","q":"What does the complement technique say, which flip proves it, and what is the co in cosine?","a":"cos(π/2 − θ) = sin θ and sin(π/2 − θ) = cos θ; the flip across y = x swaps the coordinates and sends the point at θ to the point at π/2 − θ; the cosine of θ is the sine of the complement, what θ lacks of a quarter turn","w":["cos(π/2 − θ) = −sin θ; the flip over the floor; the co stands for the coordinate","cos(π/2 − θ) = cos θ − π/2; no flip is needed; the co stands for the corner","cos(π − θ) = sin θ; the flip across the upright axis; the complement is what θ lacks of a half turn"],"why":"F(cos θ, sin θ) = (sin θ, cos θ)."},
 {"u":"21e4.1","q":"What does the half-turn technique say, which moves prove its two pairs, and what is a reference angle?","a":"cos(π − θ) = −cos θ and sin(π − θ) = sin θ, by the flip across the upright axis; cos(θ + π) = −cos θ and sin(θ + π) = −sin θ, by the half turn; a reference angle is the first-quarter arc from which an arc's cos and sin are read, up to signs","w":["cos(π − θ) = cos θ and sin(π − θ) = −sin θ, by the flip over the floor; a reference angle is π/2","both pairs come from the half turn; a reference angle is the arc from (0, 1)","cos(θ + π) = cos θ and sin(θ + π) = sin θ, by the whole turn; a reference angle is any angle in the upper half"],"why":"So tan(θ + π) = tan θ: tan repeats every half turn."},
 {"u":"21e5.1","q":"Why are \"sin θ = sin φ, so θ = φ\" and \"tan(θ + π) = −tan θ\" moves to watch out for?","a":"equal sines also allow φ = π − θ, and whole turns; and in tan(θ + π) both coordinates change sign, so their ratio does not","w":["equal sines force θ = −φ; tan(θ + π) is not defined","equal sines allow φ = θ + π; tan(θ + π) = −tan θ holds on the upper half","equal sines force θ = φ on the whole line; only cos changes sign in tan(θ + π)"],"why":"sin(π − θ) = sin θ, and tan(θ + π) = (−sin θ)/(−cos θ)."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["21f"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["21f"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"21f1.1","q":"What does the upward-arc technique say, and why is its integral proper?","a":"for 0 ≤ h < π/2, h = ∫₀^{sin h} ds/√(1 − s²): the flip across y = x carries the arc from (1, 0) onto the arc from (0, 1) to (sin h, cos h); sin h < 1, so the bottom is never 0","w":["h = ∫₀^{cos h} ds/√(1 − s²); it is proper because cos h > 0","h = ∫₀^{sin h} √(1 − s²) ds; it is proper because the top is bounded","h = ∫_{sin h}^1 ds/√(1 − s²); it is proper because the flip removes the end at 1"],"why":"The flip keeps lengths (21d1), and the image arc is the graph of √(1 − s²) for 0 ≤ s ≤ sin h."},
 {"u":"21f2.1","q":"What does the small-arc technique say, and where do its two fences come from?","a":"for 0 ≤ h < π/2, sin h ≤ h ≤ tan h; on [0, sin h] the integrand is at least 1 and at most 1/cos h, so h lies between the boxes sin h · 1 and sin h · (1/cos h)","w":["tan h ≤ h ≤ sin h; the fences come from the lower and upper halves of the circle","sin h ≤ h ≤ tan h; the fences come from measuring a small arc with a ruler","cos h ≤ h ≤ 1; the integrand is between cos h and 1"],"why":"The height is never more than the walk, and the walk never more than the height on the touching line."},
 {"u":"21f3.1","q":"What does the sine-over-arc technique say, how is sin h / h squeezed, and what does the proof give in passing?","a":"sin h / h heads to 1 as h heads to 0; dividing the fences by h and turning them over puts it between cos h and 1, and cos h heads to 1; in passing, sin and cos are continuous at 0","w":["sin h / h heads to 0; it is squeezed between 0 and h; in passing, sin 0 = 0","sin h / h heads to 1; it is squeezed between 1 and tan h, which heads to 1; in passing, tan is continuous everywhere","sin h / h equals 1 for small h; no squeeze is needed; in passing, sin h = h"],"why":"cos h = √(1 − sin² h) is at least √(1 − h²), since sin h ≤ h."},
 {"u":"21f4.1","q":"What does the cosine-gap technique say, and what does multiplying top and bottom by 1 + cos h do?","a":"(1 − cos h)/h heads to 0 as h heads to 0; the top becomes 1 − cos² h = sin² h, so the quotient is (sin h / h) · sin h/(1 + cos h), heading to 1 · 0/2","w":["(1 − cos h)/h heads to 1; the top becomes cos² h","(1 − cos h)/h heads to 0; the top becomes 1 + cos² h, which cancels the h","(1 − cos h)/h heads to 1/2; the top becomes sin h"],"why":"It uses 21f3 and the continuity of sin and cos at 0."},
 {"u":"21f5.1","q":"Why are \"sin h / h → cos 0 / 1 = 1 by 14i's technique\" and \"lim (sin h / h) = lim sin h / lim h\" moves to watch out for?","a":"the first is circular: the technique needs the slope of sin at 0, which is this very limit; the second gives 0/0, and limits-divide needs a bottom limit other than 0","w":["the first gives the wrong value; the second is right, since both limits exist","the first is right once sin′ = cos is known from a table; the second gives 1/1","both fail because sin h / h has no limit at 0"],"why":"The squeeze of 21f3 finds the limit without any slope."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["21g"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["21g"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"21g1.1","q":"What does the slope-of-sin technique say, and which two limits of 21f carry its proof?","a":"for every θ, the slope of sin at θ is cos θ; split sin(θ + h) by the sum technique: (cos h − 1)/h heads to 0 and sin h / h heads to 1","w":["the slope of sin at θ is −cos θ; sin h / h heads to 0 and (cos h − 1)/h heads to 1","the slope of sin at θ is cos θ; both limits head to 1","the slope of sin at θ is sin θ; only sin h / h is needed"],"why":"The quotient is sin θ · (cos h − 1)/h + cos θ · (sin h / h)."},
 {"u":"21g2.1","q":"Why is the slope of cos at θ equal to −sin θ, and where does the minus come from?","a":"cos θ = sin(π/2 − θ), and the chain technique brings out the slope −1 of π/2 − θ: cos(π/2 − θ) · (−1) = −sin θ","w":["cos is sin slid by π, and the slide changes the sign","the minus comes from the circle technique, cos² = 1 − sin²","cos θ = sin(π/2 − θ), and the minus comes from sin being odd"],"why":"The complement technique is used twice, going in and coming out."},
 {"u":"21g3.1","q":"What does the slope-of-tan technique say, and which two techniques prove it?","a":"wherever cos θ ≠ 0, the slope of tan at θ is sec² θ = 1 + tan² θ; the quotient technique, then the circle technique on top","w":["the slope of tan is sec θ; the chain technique, then the sum technique","the slope of tan is −csc² θ; the quotient technique, then the complement","the slope of tan is cos² θ; the product technique, then the circle technique"],"why":"(cos · cos − sin · (−sin))/cos² = 1/cos²."},
 {"u":"21g4.1","q":"What does the swing technique say, and why does the gap g stay 0?","a":"if f″ = −f everywhere, then f(θ) = f(0) cos θ + f′(0) sin θ; the gap g has g″ = −g and g(0) = g′(0) = 0, so g² + g′² has slope 0 and stays 0","w":["if f″ = −f then f = sin; the gap is 0 because sin(0) = 0","if f″ = f then f(θ) = f(0) cos θ + f′(0) sin θ; the gap is 0 because g′ = g","if f″ = −f then f(θ) = f(0) cos θ + f′(0) sin θ; the gap is 0 because g is a slide of f"],"why":"(g² + g′²)′ = 2gg′ + 2g′g″ = 0, and squares that add to 0 are each 0."},
 {"u":"21g5.1","q":"Why is \"f″ = −f, so f = sin\" a move to watch out for?","a":"every a cos + b sin answers f″ = −f; two starting numbers, f(0) and f′(0), decide which","w":["only cos answers f″ = −f","no function answers f″ = −f","sin answers f″ = f, not f″ = −f"],"why":"cos″ = −cos too, and sums and multiples of answers are answers."});
